+### Host-owned HTTP/1.1 networking for HLS
+
+`LoadOptions.hlsUsesHTTP1` is a per-load HLS networking declaration, false by default, available
+in Debug and Release. The host configures encrypted DNS on `NWParameters.PrivacyContext.default`
+before networking begins. HLS connections inherit that context; the engine embeds no provider.
+
+With true, HLS admission, playlists, keys, media, VOD/live ingest, audio tap and HLS subtitle
+renditions use TCP/TLS with HTTP/1.1 framing and no h2/h3 negotiation. The existing HLS origin
+relay supplies AVPlayer's local HLS URL. Positive HEVC-in-MPEG-TS evidence retains its ingest;
+query-wrapped segments retain one persistent same-origin connection. A required relay that cannot
+be prepared fails the load. There is no recovery through HTTP/2 or direct AVFoundation origin
+networking. Default false preserves existing transport, subtitle and TLS routing.
+
+`HLSHTTP1Client` is a playback-scoped HTTP/1.1 client for hosts doing HLS admission before engine
+load. Its `init()` creates an empty pool; `cancel()` cancels active requests and closes pooled
+connections. `data(for:limit:prefix:)` accepts a GET and returns its bounded body and
+`HTTPURLResponse`; `prefix: true` stops at the requested prefix even if Range is ignored.
+`HLSHTTP1Client.BodyLimitExceeded` and its `limit` identify an oversized body.
+`stream(for:limit:receiveResponse:receive:)` delivers a response before body chunks; returning
+false from `receiveResponse` skips the body. Cancellation closes the active connection. An idle
+framed connection can be reused, while concurrently active resources have independent connections.
+Existing credential scoping, redirect header policy, byte ranges, chunk decoding, system TLS trust
+and `EngineTLS.serverTrustEvaluator` (including its real `serverTrust`) remain enforced.
+
+The optional `EngineLog.remoteHLSRouteObserver` also reports the actual connection establishment
+DNS protocol and the HTTP/1.1 transfer/reuse events. A cache-only/unknown resolution is unconfirmed;
+it is never relabeled as HTTPS. A route selection, successful HTTP response, or advancing media
+clock alone does not prove encrypted DNS. A direct AVFoundation request remains unobservable by
+this callback.
+
